From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Micha=C5=82=20Kope=C4=87?= <michal.kopec@3mdeb.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:58:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] slaunch/txt: start the high PMR at 4G
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The high PMR base was the lowest RAM address above 4G rounded up to the
PMR alignment.  Firmware can leave the first pages above 4G out of the
memory map, e.g. RAM starting at 0x100001000, which gives a base of
0x100200000.  The RAM below that base is not protected from DMA and
Linux rejects any high PMR base other than 4G with "Error hi PMR base".

Start the high PMR at 4G and round its end up so that it covers all RAM.
Leave it empty when there is no RAM above 4G instead of relying on the
rounding of ~0 to wrap around to 0.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Michał Kopeć <michal.kopec@3mdeb.com>
---
 grub-core/loader/slaunch/txt.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/grub-core/loader/slaunch/txt.c b/grub-core/loader/slaunch/txt.c
index ec8957155..2891cec15 100644
--- a/grub-core/loader/slaunch/txt.c
+++ b/grub-core/loader/slaunch/txt.c
@@ -844,11 +844,25 @@ init_txt_heap (struct grub_slaunch_params *slparams, struct grub_txt_acm_header
   os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_lo_size = ALIGN_DOWN (grub_mmap_get_highest (0x100000000),
 					       GRUB_TXT_PMR_ALIGN);
 
-  os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_base = ALIGN_UP (grub_mmap_get_lowest (0x100000000),
-					     GRUB_TXT_PMR_ALIGN);
-  os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_size = ALIGN_DOWN (grub_mmap_get_highest (0xffffffffffffffff),
-					       GRUB_TXT_PMR_ALIGN);
-  os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_size -= os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_base;
+  /*
+   * The high PMR has to start at 4G.  Firmware may leave the first pages
+   * above 4G out of the memory map; rounding the lowest RAM address up would
+   * then leave the RAM below the rounded base unprotected and Linux rejects
+   * any base other than 4G.  Leave the high PMR empty if there is no RAM
+   * above 4G.
+   */
+  if (grub_mmap_get_lowest (0x100000000) != ~(grub_uint64_t) 0)
+    {
+      os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_base = 0x100000000;
+      os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_size = ALIGN_UP (grub_mmap_get_highest (0xffffffffffffffff),
+						 GRUB_TXT_PMR_ALIGN);
+      os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_size -= os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_base;
+    }
+  else
+    {
+      os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_base = 0;
+      os_sinit_data->vtd_pmr_hi_size = 0;
+    }
 
   grub_dprintf ("slaunch",
 		"vtd_pmr_lo_base: 0x%" PRIxGRUB_UINT64_T " vtd_pmr_lo_size: 0x%"
